Dear all,

I'm working on a set of skulls and I wish to find out to each modules my landmarks should be assigned and also how many modules exist. I've searched in the Geomorph 3.0 files but all methods I find require a modules known a priori, which allow me to test hypothesis but not to find out which landmarks are more correlated to each other. I found the function
define.modules
the closest to what I wish to do, but still it requires a priori known modules. My question is: is there any way to determine modules with Geomorph?

I checked on the methodology of  Goswami, A. (2006), which appears to be the most straightforward way to determine modules.However, one of the products of gpagen is the vaeiance-covariance matrix of coordinates. Is there a way to obtain the variance-covariance matrix of landmarks, do that in order to then be able to do something like this (see below), to find the most correlated landmarks?

data.gpa <- gpagen(Landma, print.progress = FALSE)
d <- dist(data.gpa$points.VCV, method = "euclidean")
fit<-hclust(d,method="ward.D")

Thank you, in advance, for your attention.

